// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6666 new: Inno Setup Uninstaller Created In Windows Root Folder
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6666
// Pull request author: edmundpaimon
// Change: new rule
// Title: Inno Setup Uninstaller Created In Windows Root Folder
// Sigma ID: 92b8ffe5-bff1-4db4-aff1-1065b9faa1b5
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Edmund
// Date: 2026-11-24 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1036.005
// Description: Detects the creation of an Inno Setup uninstaller ("unins000.exe" or its "unins000.dat" log)
//   directly in the Windows folder. By default, Inno Setup writes its "unins*" files to the
//   installation folder, so their presence in "C:\Windows" usually means an installer chose the
//   Windows folder as its installation folder. Legitimate software rarely installs there. In one
//   observed case, a fake "Cloudflare Setup" dropper installed to "C:\Windows" and placed a pay-per-
//   install bundle there.
// False positives:
//   - Legacy software that installs into the Windows folder
// References:
//   - https://jrsoftware.org/ishelp/topic_setup_uninstallfilesdir.htm
//   - https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/93330938600704cff0498e82dad6fa31e9954bb8eaade16d0400f33ac7301c89
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/8d298a8f8e6c8056e7371000165896024f63dbbd/rules/windows/file/file_event/file_event_win_susp_inno_setup_uninstaller_windows_root.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceFileEvents
| where FolderPath matches regex "(?i)^[a-z]:\\\\Windows\\\\unins\\d{3}\\.(exe|dat)$"
